WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.559
Context is the token sequence available to the model for the current request.

00:00:05.559 --> 00:00:10.178
It includes instructions, conversation, documents, code, tool output, and generated history.

00:00:10.178 --> 00:00:13.537
Longer context increases cache memory and prompt-processing time.

00:00:13.537 --> 00:00:16.896
Short chat and rewriting usually need modest context.

00:00:16.896 --> 00:00:19.836
Long-document analysis and summarization may need more,

00:00:19.836 --> 00:00:26.554
but retrieval or chunking can be more reliable than placing an entire archive into one prompt.

00:00:26.554 --> 00:00:30.753
Coding workloads vary from a small function to repository-wide analysis.

00:00:30.753 --> 00:00:34.113
Useful context depends on file selection, language, tools,

00:00:34.113 --> 00:00:37.472
and the model's ability to use distant information.

00:00:37.472 --> 00:00:40.411
Maximum context does not guarantee maximum understanding.

00:00:40.411 --> 00:00:44.190
Retrieval-augmented generation adds selected source passages to a prompt.

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.810
Its workload includes embeddings, indexing, retrieval quality, context assembly, and citations.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:53.429
Agents add tool calls, state, repeated prompts, and potentially long histories.

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:56.788
Vision-language models add image encoding, visual tokens, adapters,

00:00:56.788 --> 00:00:58.048
and image-memory requirements.

00:00:58.048 --> 00:01:02.247
Batch processing and multiple users multiply cache and compute demand.

00:01:02.247 --> 00:01:06.866
The same base model can behave very differently across these workloads.

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:11.065
Size context for the actual application and cap unnecessary history.

00:01:11.065 --> 00:01:14.844
Measure prompt-processing latency, cache growth, accuracy, and failure behavior.

00:01:14.844 --> 00:01:20.723
A smaller context used with disciplined information selection often outperforms an oversized noisy prompt.

00:01:20.723 --> 00:01:26.181
Define a workload envelope with typical and worst-case prompt length, output length, images,

00:01:26.181 --> 00:01:28.281
tools, sessions, and request rate.

00:01:28.281 --> 00:01:32.900
Capacity planning needs that envelope, not one carefully chosen demonstration prompt.
